 "not_in_units": {
  "note": "Reconciliation residual, not a ballot category: statewide.turnout2022 is the official SOS statewide figure (1,020,208), while Washington and Montgomery county values were replaced with EAC EAVS 2022 figures (2,266 and 9,679 instead of the SOS 2,002 and 11,168, which disagree with those counties' certified governor votes). See notes.",
  "turnout2022": 1225
 },
 "notes": [
  "turnout2022: the Kansas SOS turnout file lists Stevens 7,979 (with only 3,246 registered) and Sumner 1,403 (17,084 registered); the two values are transposed (certified 2022 governor votes: Stevens 1,401, Sumner 7,890; EAVS: 1,434 / 7,936), so Stevens = 1,403 and Sumner = 7,979 here. Statewide total unaffected.",
  "turnout2022: Washington (SOS 2,002 ballots but 2,257 certified governor votes) and Montgomery (SOS 11,168 ballots but 9,611 governor votes) use EAC EAVS 2022 F1a instead: 2,266 and 9,679. County sum is therefore 1,018,983, 1,225 below the official statewide 1,020,208 kept in statewide.turnout2022.",
  "turnout2022: in Rice (3,027 ballots vs 3,112 governor votes) and Wilson (3,107 ballots vs 3,322 US Senate votes) certified race totals slightly exceed the published ballots cast; EAVS agrees with the SOS ballots figure (3,014 / 3,108), so the SOS values are kept.",
  "sen_prev excludes unregistered write-in votes, which the official 2020 canvass does not count (shown only in large-county sheets: Shawnee 94, Sedgwick 242, Wyandotte 120); county sums equal the official statewide totals exactly."
 ],
